---
import { readFile } from 'node:fs/promises';
import {
  Badge,
  Badges,
  Button,
  BuildOutput,
  CodeBlock,
  Facts,
  Faq,
  Features,
  Hero,
  Install,
  Quickstart,
  ReleaseHighlights,
} from '@casoon/pages-theme/components';
import Base from '@casoon/pages-theme/layouts/Base.astro';
import config from 'virtual:casoon-pages/config';
import { parseChangelog } from '@casoon/pages-theme/lib/changelog';
import { url } from '@casoon/pages-theme/url';
// Animated SVGs built with castwright from site/captures/render*.terminal.yaml: real chartlet
// runs captured by site/captures/capture.sh. The narrow scene has 50 columns for phones.
import renderNarrowSvg from '../../captures/render-narrow.svg?url';
import renderSvg from '../../captures/render.svg?url';
import { exampleCount, render, spec } from '../showcase';

// Every chart on this page is rendered by chartlet during the build, from examples/.
const hero = render(spec('energy-flow'), 'hero', 'svg');
const heroMobile = render(spec('energy-flow'), 'hero-mobile', 'svg', undefined, 'mobile');
const gallery = [
  { slug: 'election-result', label: 'Parliament' },
  { slug: 'temperature-projection', label: 'Time series with bands' },
  { slug: 'service-dependencies', label: 'Network' },
].map((item) => ({ ...item, html: render(spec(item.slug), `gallery-${item.slug}`, 'svg').html }));
const diagram = render(spec('release-flow'), 'build-output-diagram');

// Skip "Unreleased": it has no version to show as "New in v…" yet.
const latestRelease = config.changelog
  ? parseChangelog(await readFile(config.changelog, 'utf8')).find(
      (r) => r.version.toLowerCase() !== 'unreleased'
    )
  : undefined;

const specJson = `{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "type": "bar",
  "title": "Monthly revenue",
  "data": [
    { "label": "January", "value": 120 },
    { "label": "February", "value": 180 },
    { "label": "March", "value": 150 }
  ]
}`;
---

<Base>
  <Hero
    title="Charts and diagrams, finished at build time."
    lead="Describe a chart in a small JSON file, and chartlet compiles it into plain SVG or an HTML figure with caption, source and data table. A chart ships no JavaScript, and the same specification gives the same bytes on every build."
  >
    <Install
      commands={[
        { label: 'npm', command: 'npm install @casoon/chartlet' },
        { label: 'cargo', command: 'cargo install chartlet' },
      ]}
    />
    <div class="start-links">
      <Button href={url('playground/')}>Try it in the browser</Button>
      <Button href={url('docs/guides/ai-agents/')} variant="secondary">Use it with an AI agent</Button>
      <Button href={url('docs/getting-started/installation/')} variant="quiet">Add it to a project →</Button>
    </div>
    <Badges>
      <Badge>MIT</Badge>
      <Badge>v{config.version}</Badge>
      <Badge tone="accent">0 bytes of chart JavaScript</Badge>
    </Badges>
    <figure slot="output" class="hero-chart">
      <div class="wide" set:html={hero.html} />
      <div class="narrow" set:html={heroMobile.html} />
      <figcaption>
        Rendered by chartlet during this build from <a class="inline" href={url('showcase/energy-flow/')}>examples/energy-flow.json</a>, with its phone variant on small screens.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </Hero>

  <section class="paths" aria-labelledby="paths-heading">
    <h2 id="paths-heading" class="cp-label">Three ways to a first result</h2>
    <div class="path-grid">
      <article>
        <h3>In the browser</h3>
        <p>Open an example in the <a href={url('playground/')}>playground</a>, change a value or a step, fix an error where the compiler points to it, and download the specification and the SVG. The compiler runs in your tab as WebAssembly; nothing is uploaded.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>With an AI agent</h3>
        <p>The MCP server <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/@casoon/chartlet-mcp"><code>@casoon/chartlet-mcp</code></a> lets an assistant draft a specification while chartlet validates and compiles it. <a href={url('docs/guides/ai-agents/#in-practice')}>Watch five real requests</a>, then run <code>claude mcp add chartlet -- npx -y @casoon/chartlet-mcp</code>. Node.js 22.12 or newer.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h3>In a project</h3>
        <p><code>cargo install chartlet</code> for the CLI, or <code>npm install @casoon/chartlet</code> for Astro and JavaScript, which needs no Rust. Every example on this site has its specification next to the output it produces: <a href={url('showcase/')}>the showcase</a>.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="status-note">
      <strong>Status:</strong> before 1.0, so a minor release may change the specification; flow, state and class diagrams are experimental. The <a href={url('docs/reference/support/')}>support matrix</a> says what is verified and what is only designed.
      <strong>"No browser JavaScript"</strong> means a chart: static SVG and HTML figures carry no script. The interactive module and the playground are optional JavaScript that a chart never loads.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cli" aria-labelledby="cli-heading">
    <div class="cli-text">
      <h2 id="cli-heading">Quiet when it works, exact when it does not</h2>
      <p>
        A valid specification renders without a word. Invalid input is refused with a code and
        the path of the field, and the exit code tells CI. A compromise such as a canvas that is
        too small still renders, with a warning that says what to change.
      </p>
      <p>
        <code>--strict</code> turns warnings into failures, and <code>--preview</code> shows
        every save on a local page.
      </p>
      <div class="links">
        <Button href={url('docs/guides/warnings-and-errors/')} variant="secondary">Warnings and errors</Button>
        <Button href={url('docs/reference/cli/')} variant="quiet">CLI reference →</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <picture>
      <source media="(max-width: 640px)" srcset={renderNarrowSvg} width="444" height="332" />
      <img
        class="terminal-demo"
        src={renderSvg}
        width="561.6"
        height="350"
        loading="lazy"
        alt="Terminal, real chartlet 0.21.0 runs. chartlet render energy-flow.json -o sankey.svg prints nothing, and ls lists sankey.svg. duplicate.json has the label January twice; chartlet render duplicate.json fails with 'chartlet: duplicate_label at /data/1/label: labels must be unique in a single-series chart', and echo $? prints 1. chartlet render narrow-flow.json renders with 'warning[canvas_too_small] at /width: the diagram needs 499 pixels here and was drawn that large; raise width to 499, shorten labels, or try the other orientation'."
      />
    </picture>
  </section>

  <section class="gallery" aria-labelledby="gallery-heading">
    <div class="head">
      <h2 id="gallery-heading" class="cp-label">From the showcase</h2>
      <a href={url('showcase/')}>All {exampleCount} examples →</a>
    </div>
    <div class="grid">
      {
        gallery.map((item) => (
          <a class="card" href={url(`showcase/${item.slug}/`)}>
            <div set:html={item.html} />
            <span>{item.label}</span>
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <Facts
    items={[
      { value: '0', label: 'bytes of chart JavaScript in the browser' },
      { value: String(exampleCount), label: 'examples, also the golden-file test inputs' },
      { value: '4.5:1', label: 'minimum contrast of every series colour on white' },
      { value: '7', label: 'kinds of diagram, laid out by chartlet' },
    ]}
  />

  <Features
    items={[
      {
        title: 'Accessible by default',
        body: 'Every chart carries a title and a description generated from the data, and the HTML output always includes the complete data as a table.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Deterministic and honest',
        body: 'The same specification produces the same bytes, so charts can be reviewed, diffed and cached. Problems come with a code and a path instead of happening silently.',
      },
      {
        title: 'Diagrams laid out for you',
        body: 'Sequence, flow, state, architecture, class, tree and network diagrams come from what is connected. chartlet places the boxes, routes the edges and keeps the main path straight.',
      },
    ]}
  />

  <BuildOutput
    caption="Diagrams too: this flow chart is laid out by chartlet from steps and edges alone. Click a step, or pick one under Focus, to see its neighbourhood."
    file="examples/release-flow.json"
  >
    <Fragment set:html={diagram.html} />
  </BuildOutput>

  <Quickstart
    lead="From a specification to a finished chart. The CLI, the Astro component and the JavaScript API are covered in the documentation."
    steps={[
      'Install the CLI, or the npm package for Astro and JavaScript.',
      'Describe the chart in a small JSON specification.',
      'Render it with <code>chartlet render spec.json -o chart.svg</code> or the <code>&lt;Chart&gt;</code> component.',
    ]}
  >
    <CodeBlock code={specJson} lang="json" title="spec.json" label="Specification" />
  </Quickstart>

  {latestRelease && <ReleaseHighlights release={latestRelease} />}

  <Faq
    items={[
      {
        q: 'What does chartlet take as input?',
        a: 'A small, declarative JSON specification — the type of chart or diagram, the data, and optional styling. chartlet validates it against a public schema and compiles it to SVG or an HTML figure; it never runs in the browser.',
      },
      {
        q: 'How is this different from a JavaScript charting library?',
        a: 'There is no chart script to ship or hydrate. chartlet renders at build time to plain SVG or an HTML figure with a caption, a generated description and a data table, and the same specification always produces the same bytes.',
      },
      {
        q: 'What chart and diagram types does it support?',
        a: 'Dozens of chart types — bar, line, time series, scatter, box plot, Sankey, treemap, heatmap and more — plus seven kinds of software diagram (sequence, flow, state, architecture, class, tree, network) that chartlet lays out itself from what is connected.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Can an AI agent generate charts with chartlet?',
        a: 'Yes. The MCP server @casoon/chartlet-mcp lets an assistant inspect data or a request, draft a specification, and have chartlet validate and compile it — no model runs inside the server and your data is never altered.',
      },
      {
        q: 'Is chartlet stable enough to build on?',
        a: 'chartlet is before 1.0: a minor release may still change the specification, and flow, state and class diagrams are marked experimental. The support matrix in the documentation lists what is verified and what is only designed.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .start-links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin: 16px 0;
  }
  .paths {
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 24px 72px;
  }
  .path-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 24px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .path-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  .path-grid h3 {
    margin: 0 0 8px;
    color: var(--heading);
  }
  .path-grid p,
  .status-note {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 15px;
  }
  .status-note {
    margin-top: 24px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .paths a {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .hero-chart {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .hero-chart .narrow {
    display: none;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .hero-chart .wide {
      display: none;
    }
    .hero-chart .narrow {
      display: block;
    }
  }
  .hero-chart figcaption {
    margin-top: 10px;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 13.5px;
  }
  .inline {
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 2px;
  }
  .cli,
  .gallery {
    max-width: var(--max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 24px 88px;
  }
  .cli {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.25fr);
    gap: 48px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .cli {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 24px;
    }
  }
  .cli h2 {
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    line-height: 1.2;
    color: var(--heading);
    font-size: 30px;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
  }
  .cli p {
    margin: 0 0 16px;
    font-size: 15.5px;
  }
  .cli code {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 12px;
    margin-top: 8px;
  }
  /* Never scale the SVG beyond a readable terminal size. */
  .terminal-demo {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 720px;
    height: auto;
    margin: 0 auto;
  }
  .head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .head h2 {
    margin: 0;
  }
  .head a {
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  .grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: 20px;
  }
  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
  .card {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 10px;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  /* Equal surfaces in a row, whatever the height of each chart. */
  .card > div {
    display: flex;
    flex: 1;
  }
  .card > div > :global(.cl-surface) {
    display: flex;
    flex: 1;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .card :global(.cl-surface svg) {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .card span {
    font-size: 14px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  .card:hover span {
    color: var(--heading);
  }
</style>
